  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (through next Thursday)...\n\nRidge over the area tonight will bring mostly clear conditions to\nthe forecast area. There is about a 30 percent chance for fog \ndevelopment along the eastern state line from around 3am through \n9am. RRFS is showing the potential for sub one mile visibilities \npossible where fog does develop. This includes Baker, Ekalaka, and\nAlzada. \n\nRidge breaks down today as a trof approaches from the northwest. \nContinued southerly winds of 10 to 20 mph with gusts to 30 mph in \nthe eastern half of the forecast area will keep temperatures \npretty close to Thursdays highs, mainly in the low to mid 80s. \nShould continue to see plenty of sun and dry conditions today.\n\nA mainly dry cold front moves through the area Friday night \nshifting winds to the north-northwest by sunrise. Trof axis shifts\nthrough the area around sunrise, but most of the energy associated\nwith the system pivots northeastward over Alberta/Saskatchewan \nand leaves our area in the dry/breezy sector of the system. Lows\nwill range from 45 to 55 degrees, coolest in the west. \n\nSaturday looks dry and cooler, with temperatures in the afternoon\nhovering around 70 degrees. Surface high pressure will help to\nkeep surface wind speeds low, despite faster southwest flow \naloft.\n\nSunday begins a shift to a more unsettled pattern. Atmospheric\nmoisture levels climb from Sunday through Monday (2-3 SD above\nnormal) as a trof digging along the northern California coast \ngrabs Pacific moisture and shoves it this direction on \nsouthwesterly flow aloft. At the same time, low level plains \nmoisture advects into the area on return flow from the southeast. \nThis will bring increasing clouds to the area Sunday along with a \nfew showers/thunderstorms to the southwest mountains by afternoon.\nSunday high temperatures will be stunted by the cloud cover with \nmainly 60s across the forecast area. \n\nThe chance of precipitation increases Sunday evening through \nMonday, with widespread 30 to 60 percent POPs by Monday morning. \nThis ramp up in PoPs is due to the western trof sliding into the \narea and pushing a Pacific cold front through the area by mid day \nMonday. Despite the chance of widespread precipitation, the \nprecipitation amounts remain under a quarter of an inch from \nSunday night through Monday night. This is due to the upper trof \nsplitting into two pieces as it moves into our area. Should this \nsystem hold together a bit better the precipitation potential and \nforecast amounts may go higher, as there will be plenty of \nmoisture in the atmosphere to work with. Showers and clouds will \nhold highs in the lower 60s Monday afternoon. Snow levels drop to \naround 11kft Sunday afternoon, 10kft by Monday morning, and 8kft \nMonday night. This will bring the potential for at least a few \ninches of snow Sunday night into Monday night for area mountains. \nThis may impact the Beartooth Highway, so plan accordingly.\n\nGenerally west flow pattern aloft looks to keep mainly dry \nconditions and seasonal temperatures in place Tuesday through \nThursday. BT/Chambers",
